    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d647">

                <pb facs="0552" n="436" sameAs="frus:frus1912/pg436"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 322.93/5.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the American Chargé
                        d’Affaires</hi>.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.—Paraphrase.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1912-07-17">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">July 17, 1912</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>The Department has received the following telegram from the American Legation at
                    Peking: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>Request received from the Minister for Foreign Affairs that instructions
                            be given our Minister in Ecuador to use his good offices for the
                            protection of Chinese who have been attacked and have had no redress
                            from the Government of Ecuador.</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <p>Guided by Department’s No. 61 of August 22, 1908,<anchor n="1" xml:id="d647-f1"/>
                    you will, with the consent of the Government of Ecuador, use your good offices
                    for the protection of Chinese subjects and their interests in Ecuador.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Knox.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
